// ═════ closing 2026-06-30 ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// ▸ balances (each a replay of the lines above; `lots` lists the parcels that remain):
//     chase/checking 24,366.65 USD · chase/savings 8,000.00 USD
//     wise 25,542.88 EUR (basis 30,104.69 USD) and 6,133.00 GBP (basis 8,303.15 USD)
//     bcp/checking 6,957.94 EUR (basis 8,162.99 USD) · claim on sr-ferreira 2,900.00 EUR (basis 3,387.49 USD)
//   at the 06-30 prices (1.1809 USD/EUR, 1.3549 USD/GBP) the foreign holdings and the claim are worth 50,114.43 USD against a basis of 49,958.32: 156.11 USD unrealized
//
// ▸ income received, cash basis: 71,671.17 USD = 45,768.45 USD from vondel + 25,902.72 USD from tern-co, six receipts each; estimated tax paid: 3,200.00 for 2025, 8,000.00 for 2026
//   earned abroad by day (the receipt's `for` month, the days of it with `in` foreign): 38,831.05 USD (vondel 24,802.47 USD · tern-co 14,028.58 USD); earned in the US: 32,840.12 USD
//   Feb 14/28 · Mar 31/31 · Apr 30/30 · May 23/31 of the month's receipt; Dec, Jan: 0
// ▸ currency gains realized in the half, ordinary (§988): 180.87 USD: 84.60 USD on euros sold for dollars · 47.38 USD on pounds sold for euros · 47.51 USD on euros spent · 1.38 USD on pounds spent
//
// ▸ presence, 02-15..06-30: 136 days; abroad 128 (Portugal 124, the United Kingdom 4); in the US 8 (05-09..05-16). Before 02-15: the US, 45 days
//
// ▸ FEIE (Form 2555), what `us/feie` can say today:
//   foreign earned income to date 38,831.05 USD; the limit for 2026, 132,900.00 USD (verify) prorated by the 321 days of 02-15..12-31: 116,879.18 USD
//   physical presence test: 128 of the 330 full days needed in some 12 consecutive months. With no further day in the US the 330th is 2027-01-18, and the qualifying
//   period would then run from 2026-02-15. Today the test is not met, so nothing is excluded in the 06-30 view; the return will claim it later for income earned since 02-15.
//   the bona fide residence test needs a whole tax year abroad: 2027 at the earliest
//   ✗ ex-c01 [A12] a test not yet met, and its earliest day: "any 12 consecutive months" is a search over window starts of the presence DaySet, and the answer depends on a plan of presence the book does
//       not have (`lena will be in pt until ...`). v4's `days(COND, window)` counts one given window; K2 could carry a forecast of the timeline, nothing yet carries the search
//   ⇄ ex-s08 presence from bookings and location history would make the 128 an observation instead of a sum of typed statements
//
// ▸ Portugal: 124 days in the country by 06-30; the 183rd would be 2026-08-28 if she stays (tax residence there: more than 183 days in any 12 months, or a dwelling kept on 12-31: from memory, verify)
//   ✗ ex-c07 [A12] tax residence is a phase derived from presence, with a start day, not a declared fact: `lives pt` was typed on 02-15 and `us/pt` would have to derive it, backdated
//
// ▸ FBAR (FinCEN 114) for 2026, so far: the foreign accounts are wise/main and bcp/checking (chase is domestic)
//   maximum of each, in its own units: wise 28,542.88 EUR on 06-22 and 9,148.00 GBP on 06-19 (the two peaks fall on different days) · bcp 6,957.94 EUR on 06-23
//   at each day's prices: wise 41,989.01 USD (06-19) · bcp 8,209.67 USD (06-23). The aggregate at one moment peaked at 46,657.01 USD on 06-19, and crossed the 10,000 USD threshold on 01-20 (16,091.91 USD),
//   so the form is due: 04-15-2027, automatically extended to 10-15. The sum of the per-account maxima, 50,198.68 USD, is more than any moment's total
//   ✎ ex-b05 [open] `peak(wise, 2026)` is in the owner's currency at the day's price; the form wants each account's maximum in its own units converted once with the Treasury's
//       year-end rate (a param that arrives in January), and for a two-currency account the maximum is not the sum of two maxima. A view: `peak` by unit, then `value(... at param treasury-eoy)`
//   ? ex-u05 [open] Wise's multi-currency account is one FBAR account or several (the balances have separate numbers): the book cannot say, the preparer decides
//   ⇄ ex-s06 the Treasury rates of exchange (published each year-end), the ECB's daily rates for the book
//   (Form 8938 for a single filer abroad: 200,000 USD at year end or 300,000 at any time, from memory, verify; she is far below it)
//
// ▸ documents out: Form 2555, the 2026 return, FinCEN 114, and Portugal's: ✗ ex-c09 [open] none can be written (sb-c26: Axiom reports lines, it writes no form)

// ═════ index of marks ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//   id      mark  closed by  what
//   ex-b02  ✎     [A6]     the January installment belongs to the year before: `for the tax year`
//   ex-b03  ✎     [A10]    a conversion between two of my own positions, or inside one, with the fee in the source currency
//   ex-b05  ✎     [open]   the FBAR's maximum: per account, in its own units, at the Treasury's year-end rate; `peak` gives the owner's currency at the day's price
//   ex-c01  ✗     [A12]    a test over "any 12 consecutive months" that is not yet met, and the earliest day it can be: a window search over the presence DaySet (THEORY T9) and a planned presence
//   ex-c04  ✗     [A6]     a receipt has two calendars: the day money came (the cash-basis return) and the days the work was done (where: FEIE); `for` has one reader
//   ex-c05  ✗     [open]   a due day that is a function of where the person lives on that day (the 2-month extension abroad)
//   ex-c07  ✗     [A12]    Portuguese tax residence is derived from presence, with a start day, not declared
//   ex-c09  ✗     [open]   Form 2555, FinCEN 114, the return: documents out (sb-c26)
//   ex-s06  ⇄     [sync]   ECB daily rates; the Treasury's year-end rates of exchange
//   ex-s07  ⇄     [sync]   Wise's CSV (rate and fee per conversion) and bcp's export (dates and decimal commas)
//   ex-s08  ⇄     [sync]   presence as `now in` statements from bookings and location history
//   ex-u01  ?     [A12]    where she lives and where she is are two timelines; `in` defaults to `lives`
//   ex-u02  ?     [open]   no `pt` system; the US-Portugal totalization agreement (which social security applies) is a law of two systems
//   ex-u03  ?     [open]   §988(e): a personal-transaction exemption of 200 USD of gain; she holds business receipts and spends them on life (sb-u23)
//   ex-u04  ?     [open]   the rate that turns a foreign receipt into USD (spot, or the IRS yearly average) is chosen once per return, and nothing holds the choice
//   ex-u05  ?     [open]   one Wise account with two currency balances: one FBAR account or two
